const i={"d1-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to detect when heart rate increases or decreases in response to stimuli or situations. This is a foundational interoceptive skill that supports all downstream regulation.",looks_like:'Client pauses and reports "my heart is beating fast" or places hand on chest when excited or anxious.',absence:"Client shows no awareness of cardiac changes and is surprised when others point out visible signs of arousal such as flushing or heavy breathing."},"d1-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to notice when breathing becomes faster, slower, shallower, or deeper in response to internal or external events.",looks_like:`Client comments on breathing changes ("I can't catch my breath") or spontaneously adjusts breathing when noticing a shift.`,absence:"Client does not recognize hyperventilation or breath-holding until physical symptoms such as dizziness emerge."},"d1-sa1-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to detect when muscles tighten, clench, or relax across the body, particularly in the jaw, shoulders, fists, and stomach.",looks_like:'Client reports "my shoulders are tight" or "my stomach feels clenchy" and may shift posture in response.',absence:"Client maintains visibly tense posture without awareness and does not report discomfort until pain or fatigue occurs."},"d1-sa1-sg1-s4":{description:"The ability to notice when the body becomes hot, flushed, or cold in response to emotional or environmental changes.",looks_like:`Client reports "my face feels hot" or "I'm getting chills" during emotionally charged moments.`,absence:"Client is visibly flushed or pale but does not report or respond to these temperature shifts until someone else points them out."},"d1-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to notice when the body feels restless, fidgety, or agitated, signaling elevated arousal that may require regulation.",looks_like:`Client reports "I feel jumpy" or "I can't sit still" and may recognize the need to move or take a break.`,absence:"Client displays increasing motor activity (pacing, tapping, bouncing) without awareness that arousal is climbing."},"d1-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to detect when the body feels heavy, sluggish, or shut down, indicating low arousal or a withdrawal response.",looks_like:'Client says "I feel really tired" or "my body feels heavy" and can distinguish this from simply being bored.',absence:"Client becomes increasingly disengaged, slumped, or non-responsive without recognizing the shift toward shutdown."},"d1-sa1-sg2-s3":{description:"The ability to notice the urge to fight, flee, freeze, or avoid before acting on it. This is awareness of the survival impulse itself.",looks_like:'Client reports "I want to leave" or "I feel like hitting something" before engaging in escape or aggressive behavior.',absence:"Client acts on fight/flight/freeze impulses immediately, with no reported awareness of the urge preceding the action."},"d1-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to briefly direct attention to internal body sensations such as heartbeat, breathing, or muscle state without becoming distressed.",looks_like:"Client can participate in a brief body scan or check-in, reporting what they notice inside for a few seconds.",absence:'Client refuses, avoids, or becomes distressed when asked to attend to internal sensations, or reports "nothing" consistently.'},"d1-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to notice uncomfortable internal sensations without the act of noticing itself triggering panic, distress, or escalation.",looks_like:'Client reports an unpleasant sensation ("my chest feels tight") and remains calm enough to continue the conversation.',absence:"Client becomes more dysregulated when attention is drawn to body sensations, with noticing itself amplifying distress."},"d1-sa1-sg3-s3":{description:"The ability to check in with internal signals while still staying aware of and oriented to the external environment.",looks_like:"Client notices a body sensation and reports it while maintaining eye contact, staying seated, and tracking the ongoing activity.",absence:"Client becomes absorbed in internal sensations and loses track of surroundings, instructions, or social context when turning attention inward."},"d1-sa1-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to tell the difference between a physical sensation (e.g., tight stomach) and the emotion it may accompany (e.g., anxiety). This supports accurate self-report and targeted coping.",looks_like:`Client says "my stomach hurts, and I think it's because I'm nervous" rather than conflating the two.`,absence:`Client labels all uncomfortable sensations as a single undifferentiated state such as "I feel bad" or "I don't feel good."`},"d1-sa1-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to distinguish early warning signs of escalation (e.g., slight tension) from peak arousal states (e.g., full meltdown). This enables earlier intervention.",looks_like:'Client identifies that they are "starting to get upset" rather than only recognizing distress at full intensity.',absence:"Client reports feeling fine and then rapidly escalates to crisis with no reported intermediate states."},"d1-sa1-sg4-s3":{description:"The ability to notice whether arousal is building, stable, or decreasing over time. This supports knowing when to intervene versus when the system is already recovering.",looks_like:`Client reports "it's getting worse" or "I think I'm calming down" accurately, matching observable behavior.`,absence:"Client cannot describe the trajectory of their emotional state and treats all distress as static and unchanging."},"d1-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to detect when energy, engagement, or alertness has dropped below a functional level, potentially signaling shutdown or withdrawal rather than calm.",looks_like:`Client reports "I feel really low" or "I can't get going" and can identify the state as different from relaxation.`,absence:"Client appears checked out, sluggish, or unresponsive but does not recognize the state or label it as a problem."},"d1-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to tell the difference between genuine low arousal or shutdown and intentional refusal or defiance. This distinction changes the appropriate intervention.",looks_like:"Client or observer can identify that non-compliance is driven by low energy rather than opposition, leading to activation strategies instead of consequences.",absence:"Under-arousal is consistently misread as willful non-compliance, resulting in escalation cycles instead of arousal support."},"d1-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to tolerate prompts or cues from others to re-engage when in a low-arousal or withdrawn state, without becoming more shut down or reactive.",looks_like:`Client accepts gentle prompts like "let's try standing up" or "how about we start with one step" without escalating or withdrawing further.`,absence:"Client rejects all re-engagement attempts, becoming more withdrawn or oppositional in response to prompts to activate."},"d1-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to accept external sensory or social cues designed to increase alertness, such as changes in tone, lighting, movement, or activity.",looks_like:"Client responds positively to a change in activity pace, a walk, or an alerting sensory strategy offered by a caregiver.",absence:"Client resists or does not respond to external attempts to raise arousal level, remaining in a low-energy or shut-down state."},"d1-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to use physical movement such as stretching, walking, jumping, or other gross motor activity to raise energy and alertness from a low-arousal state.",looks_like:"Client gets up and moves, does jumping jacks, or walks briskly and subsequently shows increased engagement and alertness.",absence:"Client remains sedentary during low-arousal states and does not use or accept movement as a strategy for increasing energy."},"d1-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to use sensory input such as cold water, crunchy food, bright lights, or alerting textures to increase alertness when under-aroused.",looks_like:"Client splashes cold water on face, chews gum, or uses an alerting sensory tool and shows a measurable increase in responsiveness.",absence:"Client does not initiate or accept sensory-based strategies for increasing arousal and remains disengaged."},"d1-sa2-sg3-s3":{description:"The ability to re-enter a task or environment after a period of shutdown or withdrawal. This is the behavioral step of returning to engagement.",looks_like:"Client returns to their desk, re-joins the group, or picks up materials after a period of disengagement.",absence:"Client remains withdrawn after shutdown and does not return to the task or environment without significant external support or passage of extended time."},"d1-sa2-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to increase arousal to a functional level without overshooting into hyperarousal or dysregulation. This is the skill of modulating the activation process.",looks_like:"Client becomes alert and engaged after activation strategies without tipping into silliness, hyperactivity, or agitation.",absence:"Client goes from shutdown directly to hyperarousal, becoming overly silly, loud, or physically dysregulated after activation attempts."},"d1-sa2-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to sustain functional engagement once arousal has been raised from a low state, rather than immediately drifting back into withdrawal.",looks_like:"Client maintains task engagement for an expected duration after re-activation without repeatedly dropping back to baseline.",absence:"Client briefly activates but quickly returns to low arousal, requiring repeated re-engagement attempts within the same activity."},"d1-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice when fight-or-flight responses have been activated, including increased heart rate, rapid breathing, muscle tension, or the urge to act aggressively or escape.",looks_like:`Client reports "I'm getting really mad" or "I need to get out of here" at a point where de-escalation is still possible.`,absence:"Client does not recognize over-arousal until they are at peak intensity and the window for self-directed calming has passed."},"d1-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to detect that emotional intensity is increasing before it reaches its peak, creating a window for intervention or strategy use.",looks_like:"Client uses a signal, word, or card to indicate they are escalating while they still have the capacity to communicate.",absence:"Client shows no gradual warning signs and appears to go from baseline to crisis suddenly, with no identified pre-peak markers."},"d1-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to allow a trusted adult to be physically near during a period of high arousal without the proximity itself causing further escalation.",looks_like:"Client permits an adult to sit nearby, stay in the room, or maintain a calm presence during dysregulation without fleeing or attacking.",absence:"Client escalates further when adults approach during dysregulation, requiring increased distance to prevent worsening."},"d1-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to accept verbal or physical guidance from an adult during high arousal without rejecting the support through aggression, verbal refusal, or shutdown.",looks_like:`Client follows a calm suggestion like "let's take a breath together" or accepts a gentle redirection without pushing back.`,absence:'Client rejects all guidance during dysregulation with statements like "leave me alone" or physically pushes away helpers.'},"d1-sa3-sg2-s3":{description:"The ability to remain in the presence of a co-regulating adult or in the current environment during the calming process rather than fleeing.",looks_like:"Client stays in the designated calming area or remains with the supporting adult until arousal decreases.",absence:"Client bolts from the room, hides, or moves away from all co-regulation opportunities during high arousal."},"d1-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:'The ability to follow simple, concrete calming instructions such as "take a deep breath," "sit down," or "squeeze this" during elevated arousal.',looks_like:"Client follows a one-step calming cue and shows a measurable decrease in arousal indicators within a short time.",absence:"Client does not follow or respond to simple calming cues during elevated arousal, continuing to escalate despite prompts."},"d1-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to use offered regulation tools such as breathing exercises, fidget objects, noise-canceling headphones, or a quiet space when they are made available.",looks_like:"Client picks up a fidget, moves to the calm corner, or begins a breathing exercise when offered during escalation.",absence:"Client ignores, throws, or rejects offered calming tools and does not engage with any external regulation supports."},"d1-sa3-sg3-s3":{description:"The ability to gradually decrease physical intensity such as volume, force, speed, or movement amplitude during the de-escalation process.",looks_like:"Client's voice gradually lowers, movements slow, or pacing decreases over the course of a calming episode.",absence:"Client maintains or increases physical intensity throughout the episode and does not show a gradual reduction pattern."},"d1-sa3-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to remain aware of one's surroundings, including people and objects, while in a state of high arousal. This prevents accidental harm during dysregulation.",looks_like:"Client moves through the environment without crashing into objects or people even while upset, and responds to environmental hazards.",absence:"Client loses spatial awareness during dysregulation, bumping into things, knocking items over, or failing to notice hazards."},"d1-sa3-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to inhibit sudden, intense physical actions such as bolting from the room, hitting, or throwing objects during the de-escalation process.",looks_like:"Client remains in place and refrains from sudden physical outbursts even while visibly distressed.",absence:"Client engages in sudden, unpredictable physical actions during dysregulation such as bolting, striking, or throwing without warning."},"d1-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize that an unpleasant sensation, emotion, or situation is uncomfortable but not dangerous, allowing the body to stay out of emergency mode.",looks_like:`Client reports "I don't like this but I'm okay" or tolerates a mildly aversive stimulus without triggering a crisis response.`,absence:"Client treats all discomfort as an emergency, responding to minor stressors with the same intensity as genuine threats."},"d1-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to prevent the body's emergency stress response from activating in situations that are unpleasant but tolerable, keeping the nervous system in a manageable range.",looks_like:"Client experiences frustration or discomfort without fight/flight/freeze activation; physiological signs remain moderate.",absence:"Client's body responds to tolerable discomfort with full emergency reactions including rapid heart rate, sweating, or panic behavior."},"d1-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to remain physically present in a situation for a brief period while experiencing discomfort, rather than immediately leaving or escaping.",looks_like:"Client stays seated or in the area for a short, defined period despite reporting that they feel uncomfortable.",absence:"Client immediately leaves, hides, or physically removes themselves from any situation that produces discomfort."},"d1-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to let an uncomfortable sensation exist in the body without immediately trying to eliminate it through action, escape, or seeking comfort.",looks_like:'Client acknowledges discomfort ("this is hard") and continues the activity without demanding immediate relief or stopping.',absence:"Client requires immediate resolution of any discomfort and cannot proceed until the unpleasant sensation is fully eliminated."},"d1-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to remain safe — not harming self, others, or property — while enduring a period of discomfort or distress.",looks_like:"Client is visibly uncomfortable but does not engage in aggression, self-injury, property destruction, or dangerous behavior.",absence:"Client engages in unsafe behavior such as hitting, biting, head-banging, or throwing when unable to tolerate discomfort."},"d1-sa4-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to tolerate a brief waiting period after appropriately requesting help or a break, without escalating during the delay.",looks_like:"Client asks for a break and waits calmly for 30 seconds to a minute while the request is processed or the break is arranged.",absence:"Client escalates immediately if a break or help is not provided instantly after requesting, treating any delay as a denial."},"d1-sa4-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to trust that relief or help will arrive without needing it to happen urgently or immediately. This is the internal belief that support is coming.",looks_like:"Client waits with visible patience after signaling distress, maintaining regulation because they expect the adult will respond.",absence:"Client signals distress and immediately escalates as though no help will ever come, showing no trust in the support system's responsiveness."},"d1-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to inhibit serious aggressive behavior toward others, even during peak dysregulation. This is a safety-critical skill that may require override-level control.",looks_like:"Client is visibly overwhelmed but does not hit, kick, bite, or physically harm others even at highest levels of distress.",absence:"Client engages in significant aggression toward others during peak arousal, requiring physical intervention to maintain safety."},"d1-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to inhibit self-injurious behavior that would require medical or physical intervention, even during extreme distress.",looks_like:"Client is severely distressed but does not engage in head-banging, biting self, or other self-injury requiring intervention.",absence:"Client engages in self-injurious behavior during peak distress that requires physical blocking, restraint, or medical attention."},"d1-sa5-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to prevent leaving safe environments in a dangerous or uncontrolled way during periods of high arousal.",looks_like:"Client remains within the safe environment or, if leaving, does so in a controlled manner that does not create danger.",absence:"Client bolts from buildings, runs into streets, or leaves supervised areas in ways that create immediate safety risks."},"d1-sa5-sg1-s4":{description:"The ability to refrain from using objects as instruments of harm during periods of dysregulation, including throwing, swinging, or stabbing with available items.",looks_like:"Client may push materials away but does not pick up objects to throw at people or use as weapons.",absence:"Client grabs and throws or swings objects at others during dysregulation, creating risk of injury."},"d1-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:'The ability to follow direct adult safety instructions during peak arousal, such as "stop," "sit down," or "come here," even when autonomy is compromised.',looks_like:"Client follows a firm, clear safety directive from a trusted adult within a few seconds, even while highly distressed.",absence:"Client does not respond to or actively defies safety directives during high arousal, requiring physical intervention."},"d1-sa5-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to temporarily yield personal choice and autonomy when safety demands require it, accepting external control without prolonged escalation.",looks_like:"Client allows an adult to guide them to a safe location or stop an unsafe action, even though they did not choose to comply.",absence:"Client fights against all external control during safety situations, making it impossible to redirect without physical management."},"d1-sa5-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize and respond appropriately to alarms, sirens, or other emergency signals by stopping current activity and following safety protocols.",looks_like:"Client stops activity during a fire alarm and moves toward the designated area or follows the group.",absence:"Client ignores alarms or becomes more dysregulated during emergency signals, failing to follow safety procedures."},"d1-sa5-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to remain with the group during emergency situations rather than wandering, hiding, or fleeing independently.",looks_like:"Client stays with classmates and staff during fire drills or lockdown procedures.",absence:"Client separates from the group during emergencies, hides in unsafe locations, or runs in unpredictable directions."},"d1-sa5-sg3-s3":{description:"The ability to shift from normal autonomous functioning to safety-compliance mode when the situation requires immediate obedience for protection.",looks_like:"Client quickly transitions from self-directed activity to following adult-directed safety instructions when a genuine threat arises.",absence:"Client continues autonomous behavior during genuine safety events, unable to shift to compliance mode when protection requires it."},"d2-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to distinguish between basic emotional categories such as happy, sad, mad, and scared, rather than experiencing all emotions as a single undifferentiated state.",looks_like:`Client accurately labels "I'm angry" versus "I'm sad" when asked, and the label matches observable affect and context.`,absence:`Client uses a single label for all negative states (e.g., "I'm mad" for both sadness and anxiety) or cannot label emotions at all.`},"d2-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to distinguish between closely related emotions such as frustrated versus angry, or nervous versus excited, reflecting nuanced emotional awareness.",looks_like:`Client says "I'm frustrated, not angry" or "I'm nervous, not scared" and the distinction aligns with the situation.`,absence:'Client treats similar emotions as identical, unable to differentiate degrees or types within the same valence (e.g., all negative states are "mad").'},"d2-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:`The ability to connect a bodily sensation (e.g., "my stomach feels tight") to an emotional label (e.g., "I think I'm anxious"). This bridges interoception and emotional vocabulary.`,looks_like:`Client reports "my chest feels heavy and I think that means I'm sad" or uses a body-emotion mapping tool accurately.`,absence:"Client can report body sensations or emotional labels but cannot connect the two, treating them as separate unrelated experiences."},"d2-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:'The ability to recognize recurring emotional patterns across time, such as "I always get anxious before tests" or "I feel sad on Sundays."',looks_like:"Client identifies patterns in their emotional experience and can predict likely emotional states based on recurring situations.",absence:"Client treats each emotional experience as novel and unconnected to previous episodes, showing no pattern recognition across time."},"d2-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to consistently use some form of communication — spoken words, AAC devices, visual supports, or gestures — to label emotional states.",looks_like:"Client reliably uses their communication system to identify emotions, whether through speech, pointing to a feelings chart, or using an AAC device.",absence:"Client has no reliable method for communicating emotional states, relying entirely on behavior to express how they feel."},"d2-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to select emotional labels that are understood by communication partners, rather than using idiosyncratic or private language that others cannot interpret.",looks_like:"Client uses emotion words or symbols that caregivers and peers understand, leading to appropriate responses from others.",absence:"Client uses labels that are meaningful to them but not interpretable by others, leading to miscommunication about emotional states."},"d2-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to identify specific events, interactions, or changes that occurred just before an emotional shift, forming the basis for trigger awareness.",looks_like:'Client reports "I got upset after he took my turn" or "I started feeling anxious when the schedule changed."',absence:'Client reports emotional shifts as coming "out of nowhere" and cannot identify any precipitating event even with prompting.'},"d2-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to recognize that triggers can come from the environment (noise, crowds), social interactions (teasing, exclusion), or internal states (hunger, fatigue).",looks_like:'Client can identify triggers across categories: "the noise bothered me," "I was already tired," "she said something mean."',absence:"Client only recognizes one category of trigger (usually social) and misses environmental or internal antecedents."},"d2-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:'The ability to connect a specific trigger event to the resulting emotional outcome, understanding "X happened, so I felt Y."',looks_like:'Client explains "when the teacher changed the assignment, I felt frustrated because I had already started."',absence:"Client can name triggers and emotions separately but cannot draw a causal line between them."},"d2-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to distinguish between the triggering event and the emotional or behavioral reaction to it, recognizing that these are separate components.",looks_like:'Client says "the trigger was losing the game, and my reaction was to throw the pieces" — separating event from response.',absence:'Client merges trigger and reaction into a single narrative ("he made me throw things") without separating cause from effect.'},"d2-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to anticipate that entering a familiar triggering context will likely produce a similar emotional response, based on past experience.",looks_like:'Client says "I might get upset at the assembly because it was loud last time" before entering the situation.',absence:"Client enters known triggering situations with no anticipation of difficulty and is repeatedly caught off-guard by predictable reactions."},"d2-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to use memories of past difficulties to forecast that a similar situation may be challenging, enabling proactive planning.",looks_like:"Client requests supports or accommodations before entering a situation they know has been hard for them previously.",absence:"Client does not reference past experience when approaching similar situations and does not anticipate or prepare for known challenges."},"d2-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize when one does not have enough information, when instructions are unclear, or when understanding has broken down.",looks_like:`Client says "I don't understand what you mean" or "can you explain that again?" when genuinely confused.`,absence:"Client proceeds with tasks despite not understanding, guesses randomly, or stares blankly without signaling confusion."},"d2-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to tell the difference between being confused (cognitive state) and being defiant or bored (motivational states), allowing for accurate self-report.",looks_like:`Client reports "I'm confused" when stuck on a problem rather than saying "I don't want to" or simply disengaging.`,absence:"Client's confusion is consistently misidentified (by self or others) as refusal or boredom, leading to mismatched interventions."},"d2-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:`The ability to distinguish between "this is hard but I understand it" (effortful) and "I don't understand what to do" (confused), enabling more accurate help-seeking.`,looks_like:`Client reports "I know what to do, it's just hard" or "I don't even know where to start" — and the report matches the situation.`,absence:`Client uses "I can't" for both effortful and confusing tasks without distinguishing between the two states.`},"d2-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to separate the feeling of being overwhelmed (too much, too fast) from a lack of interest (not engaging), as these require different responses.",looks_like:`Client says "there's too much happening" rather than "this is boring" when actually overwhelmed, or vice versa.`,absence:`Client conflates overwhelm and disinterest, saying "I don't care" when actually flooded or "it's too much" when actually bored.`},"d2-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to detect confusion early enough in the process to communicate it before it leads to frustration, shutdown, or behavioral escalation.",looks_like:"Client raises hand, uses a signal card, or verbally states confusion within the first moments of encountering a problem.",absence:"Client sits silently with growing confusion until it converts to frustration or behavioral outburst, missing the window to signal."},"d2-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to keep confusion as a cognitive problem rather than letting it cascade into emotional dysregulation, preserving the ability to problem-solve.",looks_like:`Client stays regulated while confused, saying "I don't get it" calmly and waiting for help without escalating.`,absence:"Client's confusion rapidly triggers emotional dysregulation — tears, anger, refusal — making it impossible to address the original cognitive gap."},"d2-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice when one is becoming fatigued, overwhelmed, or depleted, recognizing that internal resources for engagement are running low.",looks_like:`Client reports "I'm getting really tired" or "I can't focus anymore" before performance completely breaks down.`,absence:"Client pushes past depletion without awareness, resulting in sudden collapse, meltdown, or complete disengagement without warning."},"d2-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to notice when the quality of one's effort, work, or behavior is declining, signaling that capacity is diminishing.",looks_like:`Client says "I'm making more mistakes now" or "my work is getting sloppy" and recognizes this as a sign of reduced capacity.`,absence:"Client does not notice declining performance and either continues producing low-quality work or is surprised when errors are pointed out."},"d2-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:`The ability to distinguish between "I can't do this right now" (temporary, state-dependent limit) and "I can't do this at all" (absolute limit), allowing for more accurate self-assessment.`,looks_like:`Client says "I can't do this right now, maybe after a break" rather than "I'll never be able to do this."`,absence:`Client treats all current difficulty as permanent inability, saying "I can't" globally rather than recognizing temporary states.`},"d2-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to accept that one's capacity is reduced without it triggering an identity crisis or shame response that worsens functioning.",looks_like:'Client acknowledges "I need a break" or "this is too much for me today" without visible shame, anger, or self-attack.',absence:"Client becomes visibly distressed, defensive, or self-deprecating when limits are reached, with the shame response itself becoming a barrier."},"d2-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to maintain a stable sense of self-worth while adjusting expectations downward based on current capacity.",looks_like:`Client accepts a modified task or reduced workload without statements like "I'm a failure" or visible loss of self-esteem.`,absence:"Client equates needing accommodation with being deficient, leading to refusal of support or emotional collapse when limits are acknowledged."},"d2-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to anticipate how an upcoming situation, activity, or interaction is likely to feel emotionally, before entering it.",looks_like:`Client says "I think the dentist is going to make me nervous" or "I'll probably be excited at the party" before the event.`,absence:"Client enters situations with no emotional forecasting and is consistently surprised by their own emotional responses."},"d2-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to predict specific stress responses based on one's own history, such as knowing that certain situations reliably produce anxiety, anger, or shutdown.",looks_like:'Client identifies "tests always make me freeze up" or "I get angry when people touch my things" as personal patterns.',absence:"Client does not connect past stress responses to future situations and does not use personal history to predict emotional reactions."},"d2-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to predict how one's emotional state will affect behavior, such as knowing that anxiety may lead to avoidance or anger may lead to outbursts.",looks_like:'Client says "when I get that frustrated, I usually end up yelling" or "if I get too anxious I might refuse to go."',absence:"Client does not connect emotional states to behavioral outcomes and is surprised when strong emotions lead to problematic behavior."},"d2-sa5-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to anticipate that regulation or focus may be lost in certain situations, allowing for preemptive planning.",looks_like:`Client warns "I might have a hard time staying calm during the assembly" or "I don't think I can focus for that long."`,absence:"Client commits to activities without anticipating regulatory challenges and then loses regulation or focus without having prepared."},"d2-sa5-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to communicate needs earlier in the process based on predicted difficulty, rather than waiting until dysregulation has already occurred.",looks_like:"Client asks for headphones before the assembly starts, or requests a break schedule before a long work session.",absence:"Client waits until they are already dysregulated to signal needs, missing the window for proactive support."},"d2-sa5-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to modify plans, approaches, or strategies in advance based on predictions about how one will respond, demonstrating proactive self-management.",looks_like:`Client says "can I do the harder part first while I'm fresh?" or "I'd rather sit near the exit in case I need a break."`,absence:"Client does not adjust plans based on self-knowledge and enters all situations with the same approach regardless of predicted difficulty."},"d3-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to begin a task or activity after receiving a verbal instruction, visual cue, timer, or routine-based signal that it is time to start.",looks_like:'Client begins working within a reasonable latency after the teacher says "start" or after a visual timer signals the beginning of an activity.',absence:"Client does not begin tasks after clear start signals and requires repeated prompts, proximity, or physical guidance to initiate."},"d3-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:'The ability to begin a task even when the starting point feels uncertain or imperfect, rather than becoming stuck trying to find the "right" way to start.',looks_like:"Client starts writing, building, or working even if unsure of the exact first step, willing to adjust as they go.",absence:"Client refuses to begin until conditions feel perfect, asking excessive questions about how to start or freezing at the point of initiation."},"d3-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to transition from a state of rest, low activity, or non-engagement into active task engagement.",looks_like:"Client moves from free time or waiting to picking up materials and beginning work without excessive delay or resistance.",absence:"Client remains in a resting or passive state long after the transition to active engagement is expected, appearing stuck."},"d3-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to begin engaging with a task even when motivation is low, interest is absent, or the task is non-preferred.",looks_like:`Client starts the non-preferred task with a comment like "I don't want to, but okay" and proceeds with the work.`,absence:"Client cannot initiate any task without strong motivation or interest, requiring high-preference activities or extensive reinforcement to begin."},"d3-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to restart a task after it has been interrupted by an external event, a break, or an unexpected delay.",looks_like:"Client returns to the task after a fire drill, bathroom break, or interruption and picks up roughly where they left off.",absence:"Client cannot return to a task once interrupted and treats each interruption as if the task must start over or be abandoned."},"d3-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to resume task engagement after an emotional disruption, such as a frustrating interaction, a disappointment, or a brief dysregulation episode.",looks_like:"Client recovers from a brief upset and returns to the task within a reasonable timeframe without needing to abandon it entirely.",absence:"Client cannot re-engage with work after any emotional disruption, requiring a complete change of activity or extended recovery time."},"d3-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to keep one's attention directed toward a task over the expected duration, returning focus to the task when it drifts.",looks_like:"Client stays oriented to the task materials, makes steady progress, and redirects attention back when briefly distracted.",absence:"Client's attention drifts frequently and does not self-correct, resulting in prolonged off-task periods or incomplete work."},"d3-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to resist leaving a task before it is completed or before the expected work period has ended, even when the impulse to stop arises.",looks_like:"Client remains at the task through moments of wanting to quit, completing the expected amount of work before stopping.",absence:"Client stops working and leaves the task area as soon as difficulty or boredom arises, well before expected completion."},"d3-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to continue working on a task even when it becomes boring, repetitive, or cognitively demanding, rather than escaping at the first sign of effort.",looks_like:"Client works through difficult or tedious portions of a task, perhaps with visible effort, but maintains engagement.",absence:"Client immediately disengages when tasks become boring or hard, requiring constant novelty or minimal challenge to remain on task."},"d3-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to accept that work often involves physical or cognitive discomfort (fatigue, frustration, boredom) and to continue engaging despite these feelings.",looks_like:'Client acknowledges "this is hard" but continues working, accepting discomfort as part of the task rather than a reason to stop.',absence:"Client treats any task-related discomfort as intolerable and uses it as justification for immediate escape or refusal."},"d3-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to keep the flow of action going once a task has been started, maintaining a steady pace without excessive pausing or stalling.",looks_like:"Client moves through task steps in a relatively continuous manner, transitioning from one step to the next without long gaps.",absence:"Client stops after each step to stare, fidget, or engage in unrelated behavior, requiring prompts to resume at each transition."},"d3-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to keep a task intact as a coherent whole rather than breaking it into disconnected fragments separated by long interruptions.",looks_like:"Client completes a multi-step task in one sitting or within expected sessions without losing coherence between parts.",absence:"Client's work becomes fragmented, with pieces done out of order, long gaps between steps, or loss of the overall thread."},"d3-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to stop engaging in a preferred or absorbing activity when it is time to move on, even when one would like to continue.",looks_like:"Client puts down a preferred toy, closes a game, or stops a fun activity when told it is time, perhaps with mild protest but compliance.",absence:"Client refuses to stop preferred activities, escalates when required to transition, or continues the activity covertly after being told to stop."},"d3-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to let go of a plan, expectation, or intended sequence of events without that release causing emotional escalation or behavioral disruption.",looks_like:`Client adjusts when told "we're not doing that anymore" or "the plan has changed" with manageable disappointment rather than meltdown.`,absence:"Client becomes severely dysregulated when plans change, insisting on the original plan and unable to move forward until it is restored."},"d3-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to move between different tasks or physical environments smoothly, without prolonged resistance, distress, or behavioral disruption during the transition.",looks_like:"Client moves from math to reading, or from classroom to cafeteria, within expected transition times and with minimal distress.",absence:"Client requires extensive warning, repeated prompts, or physical guidance to transition, and may escalate during the shift."},"d3-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to adjust behavior and expectations when the schedule, routine, or plans change unexpectedly.",looks_like:'Client adapts when the schedule changes (e.g., "gym is canceled today") with brief disappointment but continued engagement.',absence:"Client cannot recover from unexpected schedule changes, becoming stuck on the original plan and unable to function in the new arrangement."},"d3-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to return to a task after being required to shift away from it, picking up where one left off rather than needing to restart.",looks_like:"Client returns to their worksheet after a schedule change or interruption and continues from the last completed item.",absence:"Client cannot figure out where they were in a task after a shift and either starts over, skips sections, or abandons the task."},"d3-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to re-engage with a task after a shift without needing a complete restart of instructions, setup, or motivational support.",looks_like:"Client picks up materials and resumes with minimal re-instruction, remembering the context and requirements.",absence:"Client requires full re-instruction, re-motivation, and re-setup after any shift, as if encountering the task for the first time."},"d3-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to keep track of where one is in a multi-step sequence while actively performing the task, maintaining awareness of what comes next.",looks_like:"Client works through a recipe, assignment, or routine and can report what step they are on and what comes next.",absence:'Client loses track of their place in multi-step tasks, often stopping to ask "what do I do now?" or staring at materials.'},"d3-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to avoid skipping steps or accidentally repeating steps that have already been completed during a multi-step task.",looks_like:"Client completes all steps in order without omissions or duplications, following the sequence as intended.",absence:"Client skips steps, doubles back to repeat completed steps, or produces a disorganized result due to sequencing errors."},"d3-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to carry out established multi-step processes from start to finish, such as morning routines, homework procedures, or cleaning sequences.",looks_like:"Client completes a familiar multi-step routine (e.g., morning routine: brush teeth, get dressed, pack bag) with minimal prompting.",absence:"Client gets stuck, skips steps, or requires step-by-step prompting for routines that have been practiced many times."},"d3-sa4-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to follow a task analysis or written/visual step-by-step guide while performing the task, using the guide as a real-time reference.",looks_like:"Client checks a visual schedule, checklist, or task analysis and uses it to guide their actions through each step.",absence:"Client ignores available task analyses or checklists and attempts tasks from memory, resulting in errors or incomplete performance."},"d3-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to manage physical tools, workspace layout, and informational materials needed for a task so they are accessible and organized.",looks_like:"Client gathers needed materials before starting, keeps workspace clear enough to work, and can find items when needed.",absence:"Client begins tasks without necessary materials, works in a disorganized space, and spends significant time searching for items."},"d3-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to set up one's environment before beginning a task so that the work can flow smoothly, including arranging materials and clearing distractions.",looks_like:"Client puts away unrelated items, sets out needed supplies, and creates a workspace conducive to the task before starting.",absence:"Client starts tasks in cluttered or unprepared environments and encounters frequent interruptions due to missing materials or distractions."},"d3-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to track what has been completed and what remains in a task, maintaining a sense of overall progress toward the goal.",looks_like:`Client can report "I've done three out of five problems" or checks off completed items and knows how much is left.`,absence:"Client has no sense of how far along they are in a task and cannot estimate whether they are near the beginning, middle, or end."},"d3-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to notice mistakes or errors while the task is still in progress, rather than only discovering them after completion or when pointed out by others.",looks_like:`Client catches and corrects errors mid-task, such as erasing and rewriting or saying "oops, that's wrong" and fixing it.`,absence:"Client does not notice errors during task performance and submits work with obvious mistakes that could have been caught in real time."},"d3-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to increase effort when a task requires more focus or energy, or decrease effort when the demand is lower, matching output to requirement.",looks_like:"Client slows down and works more carefully on challenging sections, then returns to a faster pace on easier parts.",absence:"Client applies the same level of effort regardless of task demands, either rushing through hard parts or over-investing in easy ones."},"d3-sa5-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to receive corrective feedback during a task and use it to adjust performance without emotionally shutting down, becoming defensive, or abandoning the task.",looks_like:'Client hears "try it this way" and adjusts their approach, continuing to work with a corrected strategy.',absence:"Client reacts to mid-task feedback with emotional collapse, refusal, aggression, or complete task abandonment."},"d3-sa6-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to continue working on a task after being corrected or after making an error, rather than stopping completely.",looks_like:"Client makes an error, receives correction, and resumes the task from the corrected point without significant delay.",absence:"Client stops working entirely after any error or correction and cannot resume the task without extensive re-engagement support."},"d3-sa6-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to avoid completely abandoning a task because a mistake was made, maintaining the commitment to finish despite imperfection.",looks_like:"Client makes a mistake on one part of a project but continues working on the remaining parts rather than giving up.",absence:"Client tears up, crumples, or throws away work after a single error, or declares the entire task ruined and refuses to continue."},"d3-sa6-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to separate an error in performance from one's overall worth or identity, keeping mistakes in proportion.",looks_like:`Client says "I got that wrong" rather than "I'm stupid" and treats the error as a task event, not a self-defining moment.`,absence:`Client equates errors with personal failure, making statements like "I'm so dumb" or "I can't do anything right" after mistakes.`}},t={"d4-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to categorize problems by severity, distinguishing between small inconveniences, moderate challenges, and major crises.",looks_like:"The individual responds to a spilled drink with a shrug and a paper towel rather than the same intensity they would bring to a genuine emergency.",absence:"The individual reacts to minor setbacks (e.g., a broken crayon) with the same intensity as a major problem, showing no calibration of response to severity."},"d4-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to distinguish between situations that are merely inconvenient and those that constitute a genuine crisis requiring urgent action.",looks_like:"When a preferred item is unavailable, the individual identifies it as an inconvenience and copes, rather than treating it as an emergency.",absence:"The individual treats everyday frustrations (e.g., waiting in line, schedule changes) as catastrophic events, responding with panic or meltdown."},"d4-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The capacity to notice when an emotional reaction is disproportionately larger than the actual problem warrants.",looks_like:`The individual pauses and acknowledges "I'm really upset but this is actually a small thing" or visibly modulates their reaction after initial frustration.`,absence:"The individual consistently matches high emotional intensity to low-stakes problems with no evidence of recognizing the mismatch."},"d4-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to deliberately reduce the intensity of a reaction when the problem is objectively small or manageable.",looks_like:"After briefly reacting to a minor issue, the individual self-corrects and uses a calmer, proportionate response.",absence:"The individual maintains high-intensity reactions to small problems without downshifting, even after the problem is identified as minor."},"d4-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to identify situations that pose genuine physical, social, or emotional risk to self or others.",looks_like:"The individual hesitates before crossing a busy street, avoids a visibly unstable structure, or alerts an adult to a peer behaving dangerously.",absence:"The individual approaches hazardous situations (e.g., hot surfaces, traffic, aggressive peers) without caution or hesitation."},"d4-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to tell apart situations that are genuinely risky from those that are simply uncomfortable or unfamiliar.",looks_like:"The individual tolerates a new environment without treating it as dangerous, while still recognizing and avoiding actual hazards.",absence:"The individual either avoids all unfamiliar situations as if they were dangerous, or fails to recognize real danger because they cannot distinguish it from discomfort."},"d4-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The capacity to anticipate what is likely to happen as a result of a given action before carrying it out.",looks_like:"The individual stops before throwing a ball indoors, stating or demonstrating awareness that something might break.",absence:"The individual acts impulsively without pausing to consider likely outcomes, appearing surprised by predictable consequences."},"d4-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to weigh immediate outcomes against longer-term effects when evaluating a course of action.",looks_like:"The individual chooses to complete a task now to earn a preferred activity later, rather than refusing and losing access.",absence:"The individual consistently prioritizes immediate gratification or relief without considering how current choices affect future outcomes."},"d4-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to determine whether a situation calls for emotional coping (managing feelings) or active problem-solving (changing the situation).",looks_like:"When unable to change a situation, the individual uses a coping strategy; when the situation is changeable, they take action to fix it.",absence:"The individual applies the same response regardless of whether the situation requires emotional regulation or practical action, often using one strategy for all problems."},"d4-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to assess whether continued independent effort will resolve a problem or whether seeking adult or peer assistance is the better path.",looks_like:"The individual attempts a task independently and, after recognizing they are stuck, asks for specific help rather than giving up or struggling indefinitely.",absence:"The individual either asks for help immediately without attempting the task, or persists alone well past the point of productive effort, never requesting assistance."},"d4-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The capacity to generate more than one possible response or solution when facing a problem, rather than defaulting to a single reaction.",looks_like:"When a first approach fails, the individual suggests or tries a different strategy instead of repeating the same action or shutting down.",absence:"The individual relies on a single, rigid response to problems and cannot generate alternatives even when prompted."},"d4-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to pause before acting on the first impulse and instead consider whether a more effective option exists.",looks_like:"The individual demonstrates a brief pause or thinking period before responding to a problem, rather than reacting instantly.",absence:"The individual acts on the first impulse in every situation without evidence of considering alternatives, leading to repeated ineffective responses."},"d4-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to observe environmental and social cues and modify behavior to match the current setting and its expectations.",looks_like:"The individual uses a quiet voice in a library, plays actively on the playground, and sits appropriately in a classroom without explicit reminders.",absence:"The individual behaves the same way across all settings, showing no adjustment for context (e.g., yelling in a quiet space, being overly passive during active play)."},"d4-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to change one's approach in response to feedback from others, such as verbal corrections, facial expressions, or social reactions.",looks_like:"When a peer or adult signals displeasure, the individual adjusts their behavior accordingly within the same interaction.",absence:"The individual continues the same behavior despite clear feedback that it is not working or is unwelcome."},"d4-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize when a current strategy is not producing the desired result and to shift to a different approach.",looks_like:"After trying a puzzle piece in one orientation without success, the individual rotates it or tries a different piece rather than forcing the same approach.",absence:"The individual continues to apply the same failing strategy with increasing intensity or frustration, unable to shift approach."},"d4-sa4-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to let go of a preferred or habitual approach when it is clearly not working, rather than persisting out of rigidity.",looks_like:"The individual willingly abandons a familiar but ineffective method and accepts or tries a suggested alternative.",absence:"The individual rigidly persists with the same strategy despite repeated failure, rejecting all alternatives and becoming increasingly frustrated."},"d4-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to link one's own behavior to the outcomes that followed, understanding the cause-and-effect relationship.",looks_like:`The individual can describe or demonstrate understanding of why a specific outcome occurred (e.g., "I lost recess because I didn't finish my work").`,absence:"The individual does not connect their actions to consequences, appearing confused or blaming external factors when outcomes are clearly linked to their behavior."},"d4-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to use past experiences and their outcomes to inform and improve decision-making in future situations.",looks_like:"The individual changes their approach in a familiar situation based on what happened last time (e.g., choosing to share after previously losing a turn for not sharing).",absence:"The individual repeats the same behavior-consequence pattern across situations without evidence of learning from previous outcomes."},"d5-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize when a task has broken down, when confusion has set in, or when cognitive or emotional overload is preventing progress.",looks_like:"The individual stops working and signals that something is wrong (verbally, with gestures, or through AAC) before dysregulation occurs.",absence:"The individual continues struggling silently, escalates to a behavioral outburst, or disengages entirely without signaling the breakdown."},"d5-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to evaluate whether a challenge is difficult but still manageable independently, versus whether one is fully stuck and unable to proceed.",looks_like:"The individual continues working through manageable difficulty but recognizes and signals when they have hit a true barrier.",absence:"The individual either requests help at the first sign of difficulty or never requests help even when completely unable to proceed."},"d5-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to choose communication as the response to difficulty rather than disengaging from the task or environment.",looks_like:"The individual uses words, signs, or a device to signal need instead of shutting down, walking away, or engaging in off-task behavior.",absence:"The individual defaults to disengagement (head down, turning away, ignoring prompts) when help is needed, rather than signaling."},"d5-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to identify and specify what type of help is needed, such as clarification, physical assistance, or a demonstration.",looks_like:'The individual says "Can you show me how to do this part?" or points to the specific step where they are stuck.',absence:'The individual gives vague, nonspecific signals (e.g., "I need help" with no further detail) or cannot articulate what kind of assistance would resolve the issue.'},"d5-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to identify and communicate the timing or urgency of a help request, distinguishing between immediate need and something that can wait.",looks_like:`The individual appropriately indicates urgency (e.g., "I need help now, I'm stuck") or patience (e.g., "When you have a minute, can you check this?").`,absence:"The individual treats all help requests as equally urgent or fails to communicate timing, either demanding immediate attention for minor needs or failing to escalate urgent ones."},"d5-sa1-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to deliver a help request using whatever communication modality is available and functional, including speech, AAC, gesture, or writing.",looks_like:"The individual uses their primary communication system to make a clear help request that can be understood by the listener.",absence:"The individual has no reliable method for requesting help or does not use available communication tools when assistance is needed."},"d5-sa1-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to formulate and deliver a request in a way that the communication partner can understand and act upon.",looks_like:"The listener responds appropriately to the request, indicating the message was clear enough to be interpreted correctly.",absence:"Help requests are consistently misunderstood by communication partners due to unclear, ambiguous, or insufficiently specific signaling."},"d5-sa1-sg5-s1":{description:"The ability to remain calm and engaged for a brief period after making a help request, without escalating or repeating the request immediately.",looks_like:"After raising a hand or asking for help, the individual waits calmly for several seconds or continues other work while the adult responds.",absence:"The individual escalates immediately after requesting help, repeating the request with increasing volume or intensity, or resorts to problem behavior."},"d5-sa1-sg5-s2":{description:"The ability to stay emotionally regulated during the wait period between making a request and receiving assistance.",looks_like:"The individual maintains a manageable arousal level while waiting, using available coping strategies if needed.",absence:"The individual becomes dysregulated during even brief waits after requesting help, showing increasing frustration, anxiety, or behavioral escalation."},"d5-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice and attend to internal states of physical pain, emotional distress, or cognitive strain before they escalate.",looks_like:'The individual reports or signals discomfort early (e.g., "My stomach hurts," "This is too loud") before reaching a crisis point.',absence:"The individual shows no awareness of discomfort until it manifests as a behavioral outburst, or denies discomfort despite clear physiological signs."},"d5-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to distinguish between physical pain and emotional frustration, which require different responses and supports.",looks_like:"The individual accurately labels whether they are hurt (pain) versus upset (frustration) and responds or requests accordingly.",absence:"The individual uses the same label or behavior for all discomfort, making it difficult for others to provide appropriate support."},"d5-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to differentiate between anxiety-based discomfort and boredom-based discomfort, which have different underlying functions.",looks_like:`The individual can communicate "I'm worried about this" versus "This is boring" and respond to appropriate interventions for each.`,absence:"The individual conflates anxiety and boredom, presenting all low-engagement states the same way regardless of the underlying cause."},"d5-sa2-sg2-s3":{description:"The ability to distinguish between sensory overload (too much input) and emotional upset (a response to events or interactions).",looks_like:`The individual identifies and communicates whether they need sensory relief (e.g., "It's too bright") versus emotional support (e.g., "I'm sad").`,absence:"The individual cannot differentiate sensory-driven and emotion-driven discomfort, leading to mismatched coping strategies or supports."},"d5-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to use words, symbols, or communication devices to express discomfort rather than relying on behavior (e.g., aggression, withdrawal) to communicate it.",looks_like:`The individual says "I don't like this" or uses a break card instead of throwing materials or shutting down.`,absence:"The individual's primary method of expressing discomfort is through challenging behavior rather than any form of symbolic communication."},"d5-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to match the urgency and volume of a discomfort message to the actual intensity of the internal state.",looks_like:"The individual communicates mild discomfort calmly and reserves high-intensity signals for high-intensity states.",absence:"The individual communicates all discomfort at maximum intensity regardless of severity, or communicates severe discomfort so quietly it is missed."},"d5-sa2-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to communicate discomfort in a way that does not include threats, aggression, or emotional collapse.",looks_like:'The individual states discomfort firmly but safely (e.g., "I need a break, this is too much") without yelling, hitting, or shutting down.',absence:"Expressing discomfort consistently co-occurs with threatening language, physical aggression, property destruction, or complete emotional shutdown."},"d5-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to separate the practical obstacle from the emotional reaction, identifying what is actually preventing progress.",looks_like:`The individual says "The instructions don't make sense" rather than "I hate this" when encountering a confusing task.`,absence:'The individual describes problems exclusively in emotional terms (e.g., "This is stupid") without identifying the functional barrier.'},"d5-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:`The ability to distinguish between a personal preference ("I don't want to") and a functional problem ("This isn't working").`,looks_like:"The individual can articulate whether they are objecting because of a real obstacle or because they simply prefer something else.",absence:"The individual frames all problems as preference violations or frames all preferences as genuine obstacles, blurring the distinction."},"d5-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to describe a problem in a logical order, conveying what happened first, what resulted, and why it matters.",looks_like:`The individual explains "I was working on the puzzle, then the pieces fell, and now I can't find the right one" in a coherent sequence.`,absence:"The individual describes problems in a jumbled, out-of-order manner that is difficult for the listener to follow or act upon."},"d5-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to provide the right amount of information when explaining a problem, without overwhelming the listener with irrelevant details or omitting critical ones.",looks_like:"The individual includes key facts and leaves out tangential information, providing a concise and useful account of the issue.",absence:"The individual either provides excessive, unfocused detail that obscures the core problem, or gives so little information that the listener cannot understand the situation."},"d5-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to remain focused on the problem being discussed without drifting to unrelated topics or tangential complaints.",looks_like:"The individual explains a specific problem and answers follow-up questions about that same issue without veering off topic.",absence:"The individual begins explaining one problem but shifts to unrelated grievances, making it difficult to address the original issue."},"d5-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to notice when an explanation is not being understood and to rephrase, add details, or try a different approach to make it clearer.",looks_like:"When the listener looks confused or asks for clarification, the individual restates the problem using different words or additional context.",absence:"The individual repeats the same unclear explanation verbatim or gives up entirely when the listener does not immediately understand."},"d5-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice when one's own preferences or goals differ from those of another person in the same situation.",looks_like:'The individual acknowledges "You want to play tag, but I want to play soccer" rather than assuming everyone shares their preference.',absence:"The individual either does not notice that others have different preferences or treats any difference as a personal affront."},"d5-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize that a solution may partially satisfy both parties even if neither gets everything they want.",looks_like:"The individual accepts a solution where they get part of what they want and acknowledges the other person also got something.",absence:"The individual views any outcome short of full satisfaction as a total loss, rejecting partial solutions outright."},"d5-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to accept a negotiated outcome that involves giving up something in exchange for something else.",looks_like:`The individual agrees to a compromise (e.g., "I'll go second if I get extra time") and follows through without resentment.`,absence:"The individual refuses all compromises, insisting on their original demand and escalating if it is not fully met."},"d5-sa4-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to remain emotionally regulated and continue productive discussion while navigating a disagreement.",looks_like:"The individual continues to use a conversational tone, listen, and propose solutions even when the other person disagrees with them.",absence:"The individual escalates to yelling, crying, or withdrawal as soon as disagreement occurs, ending the negotiation process."},"d5-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize when a situation requires accommodation, modification, or boundary-setting to protect one's well-being or ability to participate.",looks_like:'The individual identifies and communicates a need such as "I need to sit closer to hear" or "I need a break before I get too upset."',absence:"The individual endures situations that exceed their capacity without recognizing or signaling that accommodation would help."},"d5-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to state needs or boundaries directly and firmly without resorting to aggression, threats, or passive communication.",looks_like:`The individual says "Please stop, I don't like that" or "I need the noise to stop" in a clear, assertive tone.`,absence:"The individual either cannot express needs at all, expresses them so passively they are overlooked, or expresses them with hostility that undermines the message."},"d5-sa5-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to maintain composure when an advocacy request is denied, delayed, or met with pushback.",looks_like:'The individual accepts a "not right now" response to a request for accommodation without melting down, while potentially re-raising it later.',absence:"The individual collapses emotionally or behaviorally the moment a need or request is denied, unable to tolerate any resistance."},"d5-sa5-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to continue advocating for a legitimate need through appropriate channels even when initial attempts are unsuccessful.",looks_like:'After being told "no," the individual asks again at an appropriate time, seeks a different person, or offers additional rationale.',absence:"The individual either gives up entirely after one refusal or escalates to disruptive behavior rather than persisting appropriately."},"d5-sa6-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice when a message has been misunderstood or when a communication exchange has gone wrong, based on the other person's reaction.",looks_like:`The individual notices a confused expression or unexpected response and says something like "Wait, that's not what I meant."`,absence:"The individual does not notice when they have been misunderstood, continuing as if the communication was successful even when the listener is clearly confused or upset."},"d5-sa6-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to restate a message using different words, examples, or modalities when the original message was not understood.",looks_like:'The individual rephrases a confusing statement (e.g., shifting from "I want the thing" to "I mean the red marker on the shelf").',absence:"The individual repeats the same words louder or with more frustration rather than attempting to rephrase or clarify."},"d5-sa6-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to identify and address incorrect assumptions the listener may have formed, correcting the record without blame.",looks_like:'The individual says "I think you thought I meant X, but I actually meant Y" to clear up a misunderstanding.',absence:"The individual does not attempt to correct misinterpretations, either accepting the wrong assumption or becoming frustrated without clarifying."},"d5-sa6-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to acknowledge that one's own communication contributed to a misunderstanding, without excessive self-blame or defensiveness.",looks_like:`The individual says "Sorry, I didn't explain that well" and moves to repair the exchange.`,absence:"The individual blames the listener entirely for the misunderstanding, or becomes overwhelmed with shame and shuts down rather than addressing the issue."},"d5-sa6-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to work with the communication partner to get back on the same page after a breakdown, restoring functional exchange.",looks_like:'After a miscommunication, the individual checks for understanding ("Does that make sense now?") and resumes productive interaction.',absence:"After a miscommunication, the individual withdraws from the interaction entirely or remains stuck in the misunderstanding without attempting to move forward."},"d6-sa0-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to orient toward another person when they initiate a bid for shared attention, such as calling the individual's name, tapping them, or making eye contact.",looks_like:"The individual turns toward a communication partner who says their name or gestures for attention, establishing a brief moment of shared focus.",absence:"The individual does not orient or respond when others attempt to gain their attention, continuing their current activity as if no bid occurred."},"d6-sa0-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to shift gaze or attention in the direction another person is pointing or looking, arriving at the same referent the partner is indicating.",looks_like:"When an adult points to an object or looks toward something, the individual follows the gesture or gaze and attends to the same item or event.",absence:"The individual does not follow points or gaze shifts, either looking at the pointing hand itself or continuing to focus on their own activity."},"d6-sa0-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to direct another person's attention to an object or event for the purpose of sharing interest, not just requesting. This is a hallmark social-communicative milestone.",looks_like:"The individual points to something interesting (e.g., an airplane, a dog) and looks back at the communication partner to confirm shared attention.",absence:"The individual does not spontaneously direct others' attention to items or events of interest, or only points instrumentally to request desired objects."},"d6-sa0-sg1-s4":{description:"The ability to shift attention fluidly between a communication partner and an object or activity, maintaining a triadic connection among self, other, and referent.",looks_like:"During play or exploration, the individual looks back and forth between the object and the partner, sharing reactions and checking the partner's response.",absence:"The individual focuses exclusively on either the object or the person but does not coordinate attention between both within the same interaction."},"d6-sa0-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to remain engaged in a back-and-forth interaction with a partner for at least two exchanges, demonstrating basic reciprocity.",looks_like:"The individual takes a turn, waits for the partner's turn, and responds again, sustaining a brief interactive loop such as rolling a ball back and forth.",absence:"The individual disengages after a single exchange or does not respond to the partner's turn, failing to sustain even brief reciprocal interaction."},"d6-sa0-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to look at or attend to a partner's reaction during a shared activity, using their emotional or behavioral response as social information.",looks_like:"The individual glances at the partner's face after an event (e.g., a tower falling) to check their reaction before deciding how to respond.",absence:"The individual does not reference the partner's reaction during shared activities, proceeding without regard for the partner's emotional or social cues."},"d6-sa0-sg2-s3":{description:"The ability to adjust one's own actions during a shared activity in response to the partner's level of engagement, interest, or disengagement.",looks_like:"The individual slows down, changes the activity, or increases bids for attention when they notice a partner losing interest or becoming disengaged.",absence:"The individual continues the same actions regardless of whether the partner is engaged, bored, or has stopped participating entirely."},"d6-sa0-sg2-s4":{description:"The ability to build play sequences collaboratively with a partner, where both individuals contribute ideas and actions that extend the shared narrative or activity.",looks_like:"The individual adds to a partner's play idea (e.g., partner builds a road, individual adds a bridge) and accepts the partner's additions in return.",absence:"The individual either plays in parallel without integrating the partner's contributions, or rigidly controls the play sequence without allowing partner input."},"d6-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:"The foundational understanding that other people have their own thoughts, feelings, and experiences that may differ from one's own.",looks_like:`The individual demonstrates awareness that another person may feel differently about a situation (e.g., "She's sad even though I think it's funny").`,absence:"The individual consistently assumes others share their preferences, knowledge, or emotional state, showing no recognition that internal experiences differ across people."},"d6-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to make reasonable guesses about what another person is thinking, feeling, or intending based on observable cues such as facial expressions, tone, context, and behavior.",looks_like:"The individual correctly identifies that a peer is frustrated based on their furrowed brow and sharp tone, and adjusts their own behavior accordingly.",absence:"The individual cannot read social cues to infer others' emotions or intentions, misinterpreting or ignoring signals about what others are thinking or feeling."},"d6-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to change one's initial assumptions about what another person thinks or feels when new information or feedback becomes available.",looks_like:`The individual revises their understanding after learning new context (e.g., "Oh, he wasn't being mean, he was just in a hurry").`,absence:"The individual holds onto initial assumptions about others' intent regardless of corrective feedback or new evidence."},"d6-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to observe and identify the unspoken behavioral expectations that govern a particular setting, such as a classroom, store, or playground.",looks_like:"The individual enters a new environment, observes what others are doing, and matches their behavior to the setting (e.g., whispering in a library).",absence:"The individual behaves the same way in every environment, showing no awareness that different settings have different behavioral expectations."},"d6-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to apply social rules learned in one environment to similar environments, recognizing that the same principle may apply even in a different context.",looks_like:'The individual applies "indoor voice" rules at a new building without needing to be explicitly taught the rule again for that specific location.',absence:"The individual treats every new environment as if social rules do not apply until explicitly taught, unable to transfer learned expectations."},"d6-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to adjust behavior to meet social expectations without feeling that doing so erases one's preferences, identity, or autonomy.",looks_like:"The individual modifies their behavior for a setting (e.g., being quieter at a ceremony) while still feeling like themselves and returning to their preferred style afterward.",absence:"The individual either refuses to adjust behavior in any context, or adjusts so completely that they lose their sense of self and become distressed by the masking effort."},"d6-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to read verbal and nonverbal cues that signal when it is appropriate to begin speaking and when to stop, in both conversation and structured activities.",looks_like:"The individual waits for a natural pause before speaking, notices when the other person has finished their thought, and enters the conversation smoothly.",absence:"The individual frequently interrupts, talks over others, or misses cues that it is their turn to speak, resulting in disjointed interactions."},"d6-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to hold back the urge to speak, act, or take a turn before the appropriate moment, even when motivated or excited.",looks_like:"The individual visibly holds back (e.g., hand raised, body still) during a group activity and waits for their designated turn.",absence:"The individual acts or speaks out of turn, unable to delay the impulse even when they know the rule, particularly when excited or eager."},"d6-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to rejoin a conversation or group activity after a pause without disrupting the flow or drawing negative attention.",looks_like:"The individual re-enters a group conversation by listening first and then contributing a relevant comment at an appropriate moment.",absence:"The individual re-enters by interrupting, changing the topic abruptly, or making their return the center of attention, disrupting the group."},"d6-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to understand that rules apply to everyone equally, including oneself, and that consistency is a feature of fair systems.",looks_like:"The individual follows a rule without protest when they see that the same rule is being applied to others in the same way.",absence:"The individual expects special exceptions for themselves while accepting that others must follow rules, or protests rules as unfair despite equal application."},"d6-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to tolerate short-term situations where things are not perfectly equal, understanding that balance is maintained over time rather than in every moment.",looks_like:"The individual accepts that a peer goes first today, understanding they will go first next time, without a significant behavioral reaction.",absence:"The individual protests immediately whenever any perceived imbalance occurs, insisting on exact equality in every moment."},"d6-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to recognize that an outcome can be fair even if it is not one's preferred outcome, separating personal disappointment from systemic unfairness.",looks_like:"The individual expresses disappointment about not being chosen but acknowledges the selection process was fair.",absence:'The individual labels every unfavorable outcome as "unfair" regardless of whether the process was actually equitable, personalizing neutral events.'},"d6-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to detect when a social interaction has been damaged, noticing signs such as withdrawal, discomfort, or tension in another person.",looks_like:"The individual notices that a peer has stopped talking to them, looks upset, or has pulled away, and identifies that something went wrong in the interaction.",absence:"The individual does not notice when their actions have caused a social rupture, continuing as if nothing happened despite visible distress in the other person."},"d6-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to name the effect one's actions had on another person without immediately defending, justifying, or minimizing the behavior.",looks_like:`The individual says "I hurt your feelings when I said that" or "That made you upset" without adding "but you deserved it" or "but I didn't mean to."`,absence:"The individual immediately justifies or denies the impact of their actions, or cannot acknowledge that their behavior affected another person at all."},"d6-sa5-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to take a concrete step toward restoring a relationship after a social rupture, such as apologizing, problem-solving, or changing the behavior that caused harm.",looks_like:"The individual approaches the affected person and offers a genuine apology, proposes a solution, or changes their behavior going forward.",absence:"The individual takes no action to repair social ruptures, either waiting for the other person to fix the situation or avoiding them entirely."},"d6-sa6-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to state a different opinion or perspective in a way that is direct but does not attack, insult, or dismiss the other person.",looks_like:'The individual says "I see it differently" or "I disagree because..." while maintaining a respectful tone and body language.',absence:"The individual either cannot express disagreement at all (suppressing their view) or expresses it through insults, yelling, or dismissiveness."},"d6-sa6-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to continue engaging with another person in a positive or neutral way even while actively disagreeing about something.",looks_like:"The individual disagrees about a game rule but continues playing with the peer and maintains friendly interaction throughout the disagreement.",absence:"The individual terminates the relationship or interaction the moment disagreement occurs, treating any conflict as the end of the connection."},"d6-sa6-sg3-s1":{description:'The ability to tolerate situations where two people simply disagree and neither will change their mind, without needing to "win" or force resolution.',looks_like:'The individual says "I guess we just see it differently" and moves on without lingering resentment or repeated attempts to convince the other person.',absence:"The individual cannot let go of unresolved disagreements, perseverating on the issue, trying to force agreement, or carrying resentment that disrupts future interactions."}},s={"d7-sa1-sg1-s1":{description:'The ability to produce self-referential statements that link actions to the self, such as "I did X" or "I felt Y." This is the foundation of autobiographical thinking and self-monitoring.',looks_like:"The individual spontaneously narrates their own experience using first-person statements during or after events.",absence:"The individual does not reference their own actions or feelings verbally or through AAC, even when prompted to reflect."},"d7-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to separate a specific event or behavior from a global self-judgment. This means understanding that making a mistake does not define who you are.",looks_like:`After an error, the individual says or indicates something like "I messed up on that one" rather than "I'm stupid" or shutting down entirely.`,absence:`A single mistake triggers global self-condemnation, identity collapse, or statements like "I'm the worst" or "I can't do anything right."`},"d7-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:'The skill of resisting the tendency to apply sweeping self-labels after isolated outcomes. One bad grade does not make someone "dumb"; one social conflict does not make someone "unlikeable."',looks_like:"The individual can experience a negative outcome and confine their self-evaluation to that specific situation.",absence:"The individual generalizes single failures into fixed identity statements, leading to avoidance, withdrawal, or escalation."},"d7-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to modify what one says or believes about oneself after new experiences provide contradicting evidence. Self-talk is treated as updatable, not permanent.",looks_like:`After a success in a previously feared area, the individual adjusts their self-assessment, e.g., "I thought I couldn't do that, but I did."`,absence:"The individual maintains outdated or inaccurate self-narratives despite repeated evidence to the contrary."},"d7-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to take corrective feedback from others and incorporate it into one's self-understanding without distortion or rejection.",looks_like:'When given feedback such as "You handled that well," the individual accepts it and uses it to update their self-concept.',absence:"The individual dismisses positive feedback, rejects corrections, or cannot integrate new information into how they see themselves."},"d7-sa1-sg4-s2":{description:"The skill of avoiding extreme all-or-nothing thinking patterns in self-referential statements, especially when emotionally activated.",looks_like:'The individual uses measured language about themselves even when upset, avoiding absolutes like "always" or "never."',absence:'The individual regularly uses catastrophizing or black-and-white self-statements during difficulty, such as "I always fail" or "Nothing ever works."'},"d7-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The learned expectation that discomfort or difficulty, while unpleasant, can be endured. This is a behavioral prediction based on prior experience, not optimism.",looks_like:"When facing a challenge, the individual approaches rather than avoids, indicating they expect to tolerate the experience.",absence:"The individual refuses or avoids tasks at the first sign of difficulty, suggesting they predict the discomfort will be intolerable."},"d7-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The expectation that one can recover even when the outcome of a situation is unknown. This reflects a history of successful coping, not certainty about results.",looks_like:"The individual enters uncertain situations and communicates or demonstrates that they expect to be okay regardless of the outcome.",absence:"The individual avoids any situation where the outcome is uncertain, or becomes highly distressed when guarantees cannot be provided."},"d7-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to begin a task, interaction, or challenge even when one does not feel fully confident. Action occurs before certainty, not after it.",looks_like:`The individual starts tasks or social interactions even when expressing uncertainty, saying things like "I'll try" or simply beginning.`,absence:"The individual will not initiate unless they feel certain of success, leading to chronic hesitation, refusal, or dependence on reassurance."},"d7-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The capacity to tolerate not knowing how something will turn out as a normal part of engaging with the world, rather than a reason to withdraw.",looks_like:"The individual proceeds with tasks or decisions despite openly acknowledging uncertainty, without requiring excessive reassurance.",absence:"The individual demands guarantees before engaging, asks repeated reassurance questions, or shuts down when certainty is unavailable."},"d7-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to recall and reference previous instances of coping or competence when facing a new challenge. Past mastery becomes a resource for current confidence.",looks_like:'The individual references previous successes when facing difficulty, such as "I did this before" or demonstrates approach behavior in familiar challenge types.',absence:"The individual treats each new challenge as if they have no history of success, showing the same level of distress or avoidance regardless of prior mastery."},"d7-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to apply confidence gained in one setting or task to similar challenges in different contexts. Success in math class supports approach behavior in science class.",looks_like:"The individual transfers confidence from one domain to analogous situations, approaching new-but-similar challenges with less hesitation.",absence:"Confidence remains locked to specific contexts; success in one area does not reduce avoidance or distress in related areas."},"d7-sa2-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to keep working on a task even when performance has been imperfect. Partial failure does not terminate effort.",looks_like:"After getting some answers wrong or making errors, the individual continues working without significant disruption to effort or engagement.",absence:"The individual stops working entirely after the first mistake or imperfect outcome, as though partial failure equals total failure."},"d7-sa2-sg4-s2":{description:"The skill of staying engaged after minor setbacks rather than pulling away from the task, person, or environment. Withdrawal is not the default response to imperfection.",looks_like:"After a small setback, the individual remains present and continues participating without retreating, shutting down, or leaving.",absence:"Small setbacks reliably trigger withdrawal, disengagement, or escape behavior, as though any failure signals the need to stop."},"d7-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice the internal experience of shame as it arises, including collapse responses, urges to hide, or sudden defensiveness.",looks_like:"The individual can identify or signal when they are experiencing shame, or shows awareness of their own withdrawal or defensive behavior.",absence:"Shame responses drive behavior without awareness — the individual becomes defensive, aggressive, or shuts down without recognizing why."},"d7-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to recognize when a behavioral reaction such as aggression, withdrawal, or defiance is being driven by an underlying shame response rather than the surface-level trigger.",looks_like:"The individual or their support system can identify that a reaction is shame-based, allowing for more accurate intervention.",absence:"Shame-driven reactions are misread as defiance, apathy, or aggression, leading to responses that intensify the shame cycle."},"d7-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The capacity to acknowledge being wrong about something without that acknowledgment causing self-directed hostility or identity destabilization.",looks_like:'The individual can say or accept "I was wrong" and continue engaging without prolonged distress or self-punishing behavior.',absence:`Being wrong triggers self-attack ("I'm so stupid"), prolonged shutdowns, or aggressive denial to protect self-concept.`},"d7-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to take corrective feedback and translate it into changed behavior in future situations, demonstrating that the feedback was processed and applied.",looks_like:"After receiving feedback, the individual shows modified behavior in subsequent similar situations.",absence:"Feedback has no observable effect on future behavior — the same errors or patterns repeat despite correction."},"d7-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to regulate emotional responses after making a mistake or experiencing failure, bringing affect back to a functional baseline.",looks_like:"After an error, the individual shows emotional distress that resolves within a proportional timeframe, allowing re-engagement.",absence:"Errors trigger prolonged emotional responses — crying, anger, or shutdown — that far outlast the triggering event."},"d7-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to stop repetitive, unproductive thinking about a past error. The mistake is processed and released rather than replayed on a loop.",looks_like:"The individual stops talking or visibly thinking about a past error within a reasonable period and redirects attention to current demands.",absence:"The individual repeatedly revisits the mistake verbally or behaviorally, remaining stuck in a loop that prevents forward movement."},"d7-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to identify what went wrong and what to do differently next time without turning that analysis into global self-condemnation.",looks_like:"The individual can describe what happened and what they would change, without accompanying statements of self-blame or worthlessness.",absence:"Any attempt to process a mistake devolves into self-punishment, making learning from errors functionally impossible."},"d7-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to stay motivated and willing to try again after making errors, rather than allowing mistakes to erode the desire to engage.",looks_like:"The individual continues to show willingness to attempt tasks and accept challenges after experiencing failure.",absence:"Each mistake reduces overall motivation, creating a downward spiral where the individual tries less and less over time."},"d7-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to distinguish between genuine social rejection and simple misalignment in interests, timing, or communication style.",looks_like:`When excluded from an activity, the individual can consider alternative explanations beyond "they don't like me."`,absence:"Any social friction is interpreted as personal rejection, leading to withdrawal, distress, or retaliatory behavior."},"d7-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The skill of avoiding the tendency to interpret isolated social difficulties as evidence that one is universally disliked or does not belong anywhere.",looks_like:"After a social conflict with one peer, the individual maintains engagement with other peers and social contexts.",absence:`A single negative social interaction generalizes to "nobody likes me" or "I don't belong," leading to broad social withdrawal.`},"d7-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to communicate one's genuine needs, preferences, and boundaries in social settings rather than suppressing them to gain acceptance.",looks_like:"The individual states preferences, makes choices aligned with their interests, and expresses needs even in group settings.",absence:"The individual consistently suppresses their own needs and preferences, agreeing with everything to avoid potential conflict or rejection."},"d7-sa5-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to resist chronic suppression of one's authentic self through excessive masking, camouflaging, or people-pleasing behaviors that erode identity over time.",looks_like:"The individual shows consistent personality traits and preferences across settings, rather than becoming a different person in each context.",absence:"The individual changes personality, interests, or opinions completely depending on the social group, showing signs of exhaustion from sustained masking."},"d7-sa5-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to identify and move toward environments, groups, or activities that match one's needs and capacities, rather than forcing fit in mismatched contexts.",looks_like:"The individual gravitates toward or requests placement in settings where they can succeed and feel comfortable.",absence:"The individual remains in chronically poor-fit environments without seeking alternatives, or is unable to identify what kind of setting would work better."},"d7-sa5-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to ask for needed supports or modifications without feeling that the request itself is evidence of inadequacy or brokenness.",looks_like:"The individual requests accommodations matter-of-factly, treating them as practical tools rather than sources of embarrassment.",absence:"The individual refuses available accommodations out of shame, or becomes highly distressed when accommodations are visibly provided."},"d7-sa5-sg4-s1":{description:"The ability to stay engaged in a relationship or group even when experiencing disagreement, correction, or interpersonal tension.",looks_like:"During a disagreement, the individual remains present, continues communicating, and does not sever the connection.",absence:"Any interpersonal tension causes the individual to fully withdraw from the relationship or group, treating conflict as relationship-ending."},"d7-sa5-sg4-s2":{description:"The ability to work toward relational repair after a rupture without pulling away entirely. Repair is chosen over retreat.",looks_like:"After a conflict, the individual initiates or accepts repair attempts such as apologies, check-ins, or re-engagement.",absence:"After any rupture, the individual withdraws completely and does not return to the relationship, accumulating social losses over time."},"d8-sa1-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to immediately stop whatever one is currently doing — including highly preferred activities — when an emergency requires it.",looks_like:"Upon recognition of an emergency cue, the individual ceases their current activity promptly without needing repeated prompts.",absence:"The individual continues their current activity during an emergency, unable or unwilling to interrupt preferred or ongoing behavior."},"d8-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to redirect attention from one's current focus to the safety demand at hand, overriding typical attentional patterns.",looks_like:"The individual shifts focus to the source of the emergency and orients toward safety-relevant information or personnel.",absence:"The individual remains fixated on their prior focus, unable to shift attention to safety demands even when physically prompted."},"d8-sa1-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to execute safety protocols such as evacuation routes, shelter-in-place procedures, or other trained emergency responses.",looks_like:"The individual follows practiced safety procedures — exiting the building, moving to a designated area, or following the group during drills and real events.",absence:"The individual freezes, bolts in a random direction, hides inappropriately, or cannot execute any trained safety procedure when needed."},"d8-sa1-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to prioritize physical safety over completing a task, saving a preferred item, or maintaining a routine when genuine danger is present.",looks_like:"The individual leaves materials behind, abandons a task mid-step, or exits a preferred activity immediately when safety demands it.",absence:"The individual insists on finishing a task, retrieving a preferred item, or completing a routine even as a genuine safety threat is present."},"d8-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to accept that another person or system is temporarily in control of one's actions during a safety event, without experiencing this as a violation.",looks_like:"The individual follows adult or authority direction during safety events without significant resistance, understanding the temporary nature of the control.",absence:"The individual reacts to external control during safety events as though it were an assault on their autonomy, escalating rather than complying."},"d8-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to follow safety steps as directed without stopping to negotiate, question, or modify the procedure in the moment.",looks_like:"The individual completes safety steps in order as instructed — lining up, moving to a location, staying in position — without arguing or bargaining.",absence:"The individual attempts to negotiate or modify safety procedures during execution, slowing the response or creating risk."},"d8-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to maintain compliance with safety rules even when physiologically aroused, afraid, or emotionally activated.",looks_like:"Even while visibly anxious or upset, the individual continues to follow safety instructions and remains with the group.",absence:"Emotional arousal overrides compliance — the individual bolts, freezes completely, or engages in disruptive behavior when afraid."},"d8-sa2-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to maintain safety-mode behavior for the full duration required, remaining in compliance until an authority signals that the event is over.",looks_like:'The individual stays in the designated location, maintains required behaviors, and waits for the "all clear" signal before resuming normal activity.',absence:"The individual prematurely leaves safety positions, resumes normal activity before clearance, or cannot sustain compliance for the required duration."},"d8-sa2-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to resist returning to autonomous, self-directed behavior before the safety event has been officially resolved.",looks_like:"The individual waits for explicit permission to resume normal activities rather than self-determining when the emergency is over.",absence:"The individual decides on their own when the emergency is over and returns to normal behavior prematurely, potentially re-entering a dangerous situation."},"d8-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to identify objects, spaces, or actions that pose a physical safety risk, such as sharp objects, heights, traffic, or hot surfaces.",looks_like:"The individual avoids or shows caution around dangerous objects and environments, or alerts others to hazards they notice.",absence:"The individual interacts with dangerous objects or environments without apparent recognition of risk — touching hot surfaces, running into traffic, or handling sharp objects carelessly."},"d8-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to recognize when a social or physical situation is becoming more dangerous over time, such as escalating peer conflict or deteriorating environmental conditions.",looks_like:"The individual notices and responds to gradual increases in risk, such as moving away from an escalating argument or seeking shelter as weather worsens.",absence:"The individual fails to detect progressive danger, remaining in situations that are clearly escalating until a crisis point is reached."},"d8-sa3-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to modify one's safety behavior in real time as conditions change, increasing caution when risk rises and relaxing when risk subsides.",looks_like:"The individual adjusts their behavior dynamically — moving closer when a situation becomes safe, backing away when new risk appears.",absence:"The individual maintains the same safety posture regardless of changing conditions, either remaining hypervigilant after danger passes or staying relaxed as risk increases."},"d8-sa3-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to use past experiences, including near-misses and actual negative outcomes, to inform future risk assessments and safety behavior.",looks_like:"After a near-miss or injury, the individual shows increased caution in similar future situations without generalizing the fear to unrelated contexts.",absence:"The individual does not update their behavior after dangerous outcomes — repeating the same risky actions — or overgeneralizes fear to all contexts after a single incident."},"d8-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to delay a movement, verbal response, or action when safety requires stillness, silence, or waiting, even when the urge to act is strong.",looks_like:"The individual holds their position, pauses before speaking, or inhibits a motor response when directed to do so during a safety-relevant moment.",absence:"The individual acts immediately on impulse during safety situations — running, yelling, or grabbing — without the ability to pause."},"d8-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to override fight-or-flight impulses when following instructions or staying still is the safer option, rather than acting on the automatic survival response.",looks_like:"Despite visible signs of fear or agitation, the individual follows safety instructions rather than bolting, fighting, or freezing unresponsively.",absence:"Fight-or-flight responses override all instruction — the individual runs, strikes out, or becomes completely unresponsive when highly aroused."},"d8-sa4-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to follow another person's directions during safety situations without resistance, temporarily deferring one's own judgment to a trusted authority figure during emergencies, drills, or dangerous conditions.",looks_like:"During a fire drill, emergency, or safety situation, the individual follows the adult's directions promptly — moving where told, stopping when told, and staying with the group — without arguing, negotiating, or physically resisting.",absence:"The individual resists, argues with, or ignores directions from authority figures during safety events — insisting on doing things their own way, refusing to move, or actively working against the person directing them."},"d8-sa4-sg3-s1":{description:"The ability to transition back to independent, self-directed decision-making after a safety event has been resolved, rather than remaining in a compliance-only mode.",looks_like:"Once the safety event ends, the individual resumes making choices, initiating activities, and directing their own behavior.",absence:"After a safety event, the individual remains passive, dependent, or compliance-oriented long after the threat has passed, unable to resume autonomous functioning."},"d8-sa4-sg3-s2":{description:"The ability to release the heightened vigilance and fear associated with a safety event once it is over, rather than carrying it forward into unrelated situations.",looks_like:"After a safety event resolves, the individual returns to baseline arousal and does not show excessive fear or control-seeking in subsequent routine situations.",absence:"The individual remains hypervigilant, fearful, or overly controlled long after the safety event, with the residual stress contaminating unrelated activities and settings."},"d9-sa1-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to permit a supporter to use calming strategies such as soft voice, rhythmic patting, deep pressure, or verbal coaching without resisting or rejecting the intervention.",looks_like:"The individual allows the supporter to use a calming technique — accepting a hand on the shoulder, tolerating a slow counting sequence, or remaining still during guided breathing.",absence:"The individual rejects all attempts at calming intervention — pushing hands away, covering ears, escalating in response to soothing tone, or verbally refusing any support strategy."},"d9-sa1-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to recognize that different situations require different levels of support and to adjust expectations for help accordingly, rather than seeking maximum support at all times or refusing all support.",looks_like:"The individual accepts full hands-on support during high-stress moments but works more independently during familiar, low-demand tasks without seeking unnecessary help.",absence:"The individual demands the same intense level of support regardless of task difficulty, or conversely refuses all support even when clearly struggling."},"d9-sa2-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to notice and respond to gestural prompts such as pointing, modeling, or visual cues from a supporter to guide behavior or task completion.",looks_like:"When a supporter points to the next step, gestures toward an object, or uses a hand signal, the individual follows the prompt and adjusts their behavior accordingly.",absence:"The individual does not attend to or respond to gestural cues, requiring the supporter to escalate to verbal or physical prompts for every direction."},"d9-sa2-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to follow spoken directions from a supporter during familiar routines, using verbal prompts as a guide for what to do next.",looks_like:'During daily routines, the individual responds to verbal cues like "Time to clean up" or "Put your shoes on" by initiating the appropriate action.',absence:"The individual does not respond to verbal prompts in familiar contexts, requiring physical guidance or repeated escalation of prompt level to produce the expected behavior."},"d9-sa2-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to attend to and follow visual supports such as picture schedules, token boards, first-then boards, or visual timers to guide behavior and transitions.",looks_like:"The individual checks a visual schedule before transitioning, follows a first-then board sequence, or references a token board to track progress toward reinforcement.",absence:"The individual ignores or does not reference available visual supports, requiring repeated verbal prompts for transitions and task sequences that visuals are designed to scaffold."},"d9-sa2-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to maintain task performance as prompt intensity decreases — continuing to respond correctly as support fades from physical guidance to gestural to verbal to independent.",looks_like:"The individual completes steps that previously required physical prompts now with only a gesture or verbal reminder, showing that the skill is being internalized.",absence:"Performance collapses immediately when prompt level decreases, with the individual unable to complete the task without the same level of support previously provided."},"d9-sa2-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to follow prompts from different supporters — not just one familiar person — demonstrating that prompt responsiveness has generalized beyond a single relationship.",looks_like:"The individual responds to gestural and verbal prompts from multiple staff, family members, or community supporters, not just one primary therapist or caregiver.",absence:"The individual only follows prompts from one specific person and ignores or resists identical prompts from others, indicating supporter-specific rather than generalized responsiveness."},"d9-sa3-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to indicate that help is needed using any available modality — spoken words, gestures, signs, AAC, or behavioral signals — rather than struggling silently or escalating.",looks_like:'When encountering difficulty, the individual signals for help by reaching toward a supporter, pressing a help button, saying "help," or using any communicative means.',absence:"The individual does not communicate when struggling and either persists ineffectively until frustrated, shuts down, or escalates to challenging behavior without ever requesting assistance."},"d9-sa3-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to accept help when it is offered without resisting, refusing, or becoming dysregulated, even if the individual did not initiate the request.",looks_like:"When a supporter offers assistance, the individual allows them to help — accepting guidance, accepting corrections, or letting the supporter demonstrate without pushing them away.",absence:"The individual rejects offered help through refusal, aggression, or avoidance, insisting on doing things independently even when clearly unable, leading to repeated failure."},"d9-sa3-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to identify and communicate what kind of help is needed rather than making a general request, specifying the type of support that would be most useful.",looks_like:'The individual asks for a specific form of assistance such as "Can you hold this?" or "Show me how" rather than a generic "Help me" with no further detail.',absence:"The individual either makes only vague, undifferentiated help requests or does not request help at all, leaving supporters to guess what type of assistance is needed."},"d9-sa3-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to assess whether help received was actually useful — did the support provided solve the problem, or is more or different help needed?",looks_like:"After receiving help, the individual either continues the task successfully or communicates that they still need assistance, rather than passively accepting inadequate support.",absence:"The individual cannot distinguish between helpful and unhelpful support, either passively accepting whatever is offered or rejecting all help indiscriminately."},"d9-sa3-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to speak up for a different kind of support when the current approach is not working, rather than simply enduring ineffective help or shutting down.",looks_like:`The individual communicates something like "That's not helping, can we try it this way?" or indicates through behavior that a different support strategy is needed.`,absence:"The individual either passively accepts support that is not working without feedback, or escalates to challenging behavior rather than advocating for a different approach."},"d9-sa4-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to orient attention to a demonstration and sustain focus while a supporter shows how to perform a skill, rather than looking away or attending to other stimuli.",looks_like:"When a supporter demonstrates a task or strategy, the individual watches the demonstration, tracks the supporter's movements, and attends to the key steps.",absence:"The individual does not attend to demonstrations, looking away, engaging with other objects, or failing to orient even when the supporter explicitly signals the demonstration."},"d9-sa4-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to reproduce a modeled action immediately after watching it performed, copying the key elements of the demonstration while it is still fresh.",looks_like:"Right after watching a supporter demonstrate a skill, the individual attempts the same action, approximating the core movements or steps that were modeled.",absence:"The individual does not attempt to reproduce the demonstrated action, either making no attempt or performing an unrelated action despite having attended to the model."},"d9-sa4-sg1-s3":{description:"The ability to retain and reproduce a modeled behavior after a time delay rather than only through immediate imitation, indicating the behavior has been encoded into memory.",looks_like:"The individual demonstrates a strategy or action that was modeled earlier in the session or on a previous day, without needing it to be re-demonstrated.",absence:"The individual can only imitate actions immediately after demonstration and does not reproduce them after any delay, requiring re-modeling for each occurrence."},"d9-sa4-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to take a strategy learned through modeling in one context and apply it to a new, different situation where the same principle applies.",looks_like:"The individual uses a problem-solving strategy modeled during therapy in a classroom or home situation, adapting the approach to fit the new context.",absence:"Skills learned through modeling remain locked to the original context — the individual only performs the skill in the exact setting and conditions where it was taught."},"d9-sa4-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to evaluate which models are worth imitating based on outcomes, choosing to learn from people who demonstrate effective strategies rather than imitating indiscriminately.",looks_like:"The individual preferentially imitates peers or adults who achieve positive outcomes, and avoids copying behaviors that lead to negative results for the model.",absence:"The individual imitates indiscriminately — copying both helpful and unhelpful behaviors without evaluating whether the modeled behavior produced good results."},"d9-sa5-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to express a preference when given options for how to receive support, such as choosing between two types of help, two locations, or two supporters.",looks_like:'When offered "Do you want me to show you or tell you?", the individual makes a clear choice, indicating which type of support they prefer.',absence:"The individual does not indicate preferences about support delivery, accepting whatever is offered without input or refusing all options without selecting an alternative."},"d9-sa5-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to communicate whether current support is helping or not — providing real-time feedback to the supporter about the effectiveness of their approach.",looks_like:`The individual indicates through words, gestures, or behavior that the support is working ("That helps") or not working ("That's not helping"), giving the supporter actionable feedback.`,absence:"The individual provides no feedback about support effectiveness, leaving the supporter to guess whether their approach is useful, often leading to prolonged use of ineffective strategies."},"d9-sa5-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to articulate what kind of support is most helpful in a given situation, going beyond simple preference to provide specific, useful guidance to the supporter.",looks_like:'The individual communicates things like "It helps when you give me a minute first" or "I do better when you show me instead of tell me," providing actionable strategy information.',absence:"The individual cannot describe what kind of support works for them, leaving supporters to rely on trial and error rather than the individual's own insight into their needs."},"d9-sa5-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to work jointly with a supporter to develop or adjust strategies, contributing to the planning process rather than being a passive recipient of support decisions.",looks_like:"The individual participates in discussions about their own support plan, offering suggestions, agreeing to try new approaches, and providing input on what should change.",absence:"The individual is entirely passive in support planning, never offering input or suggestions, and does not engage when invited to participate in decisions about their own support."},"d9-sa6-sg1-s1":{description:"The ability to identify and distinguish familiar supporters from unfamiliar people, showing differential behavior toward known helpers versus strangers.",looks_like:"The individual greets familiar supporters, approaches them preferentially, or shows increased comfort and engagement compared to their response to unfamiliar adults.",absence:"The individual treats all adults identically — showing no differential response between trusted supporters and unfamiliar people, or failing to recognize familiar helpers across sessions."},"d9-sa6-sg1-s2":{description:"The ability to show different levels of trust and openness with different supporters based on the quality and history of the relationship, rather than trusting everyone equally or no one at all.",looks_like:"The individual is more willing to attempt difficult tasks, accept correction, or share distress with supporters who have established trust, compared to newer or less familiar supporters.",absence:"The individual either trusts all adults identically regardless of relationship history, or withholds trust from everyone equally, showing no relationship-dependent variation in openness."},"d9-sa6-sg2-s1":{description:"The ability to recover a support relationship after a conflict, misunderstanding, or difficult interaction, rather than permanently withdrawing from or rejecting the supporter.",looks_like:"After a disagreement or a session that went poorly, the individual re-engages with the supporter next time, accepts repair attempts, and does not hold permanent grudges.",absence:"A single negative interaction permanently damages the individual's willingness to engage with that supporter — they refuse to work with them, avoid them, or remain hostile indefinitely."},"d9-sa6-sg2-s2":{description:"The ability to sustain engagement with the support system over extended periods, continuing to participate in support relationships even when progress is slow or sessions are difficult.",looks_like:"The individual continues to attend sessions, engage with supporters, and participate in the support process over weeks and months, even through challenging periods.",absence:"The individual disengages from the support system after setbacks, refusing to continue working with supporters or becoming increasingly passive and withdrawn over time."}},n={...i,...t,...s};function a(e){return n[e]||null}export{a as g};
